{ "move_object": { "topic": "EXPLAIN", "confusion": "C5" , "note": "In `Some(n @ 10..)` the outer `Some` is matched first, then the inner value is tested. `n @ 10..` means: test the inner value against the range `10..` and bind that same inner value to `n`. So for `Some(40)` the arm fires and `n` is the inner `i32` 40, not the whole `Some(40)`. The range `10..` is testing the inner integer, not the `Option`." , "highlights": [] , "response": "Look at syntax.rs lines 52 to 56. The match is on `v: Option<i32>`. The arm `Some(n @ 10..)` first requires the value to be `Some`, then it looks inside that `Some`.

`n @ 10..` is an `@` binding: the sub-pattern `10..` is tested against the inner value, and the whole inner value that passed the test is bound to `n`. So `n` is an `i32`, not an `Option<i32>`. For `Some(40)` the range `10..` is true for 40, so `n` becomes 40 and the arm prints `large 40`.

Try running syntax.rs and check which arm fires for `Some(3)`, `Some(40)` and `None`." , "confusion_skill": "C5" , "move": "POINT" } }

