{ "move_object": { "topic": "slice length is runtime, array length is in type", "move": "EXPLAIN" , "note": "Array `[i32; 3]` has length 3 in its type, so `let [x, y, z] = arr` is irrefutable. A slice `&[i32]` has no length in its type, length is only known at run time. That makes `[head, ..]` refutable because the slice could be empty. `let` needs an irrefutable pattern, so Rust rejects `let [head, ..] = items` with E0005. You can use `let [head, ..] = items else { return; }` as in refutability.rs lines 20-22, or put it in a `match` where exhaustiveness is checked." , "confusion": "" , "response": "Array length is part of the type, slice length is not.

`let [x, y] = arr` works because `arr` is e.g. `[i32; 2]`. The compiler knows the array is exactly 2 long, so the pattern can never fail, it is irrefutable.

`let [x, ..] = slice` does not work because `slice` is `&[i32]`. The type says nothing about how many elements are inside, that is only known at run time. The pattern `[x, ..]` requires at least one element, so it can fail on an empty slice. That makes it refutable, and `let` needs irrefutable patterns. See refutability.rs lines 10-11 for the irrefutable array case and lines 18-23 for the refutable slice case with `let-else`." , "confusion_skill": "" } }

